WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.509
TruTV's hidden camera prank show Impractical
Jokers has been delighting fans of hidden

00:00:05.509 --> 00:00:07.839
camera comedy since 2011.

00:00:07.839 --> 00:00:13.300
Whether you're a long-time aficionado or brand
new to the show, here are a few Jokers-related

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:15.910
tidbits you're probably unaware of.

00:00:15.910 --> 00:00:18.330
Class clowns

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:23.500
Making goofing off with your best buds from
high school into your job sounds like a ridiculous

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:28.990
pipe dream, but that's exactly what happened
to Brian "Q" Quinn, Sal Vulcano, James "Murr"

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:30.430
Murray, and Joe Gatto.

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:34.679
The long-time friends were born within nine
months of each other on Staten Island and

00:00:34.679 --> 00:00:39.960
met as teens at an all-boys Catholic high
school — the perfect proving ground for

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:42.739
fine-tuning their prankster chops.

00:00:42.739 --> 00:00:47.691
Murray says their high school shenanigans
helped shape their specialized skillset, making

00:00:47.691 --> 00:00:51.159
them "well-practiced in the art of pranking."

00:00:51.159 --> 00:00:55.879
One such early prank — Gatto's frequent
attempts to touch people with his nose without

00:00:55.879 --> 00:00:59.440
them noticing — even made its way onto Jokers.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:01.900
Who says school doesn't prepare you for the
real world …

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:05.200
"Oh my God."

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:07.580
"I feel like we're about to cause an international
incident."

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.420
Troupers

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:16.460
Goofing off is fun and all, but you need to
find an audience if you're going make a living

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:17.460
at it.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:22.219
So in 1999, the guys formed a comedy troupe
called The Tenderloins.

00:01:22.219 --> 00:01:27.990
Fast-forward to 2007 when the four friends
entered the Carson Daly-curated NBC online

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.310
comedy video competition It's Your Show.

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:36.640
Their entry, "Time Thugs," an infomercial
spoof about some time-hopping goons that can

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:40.800
fix your past, helped nab them the top prize
of $100,000 …

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.180
"You Timmy Jenkins?"

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:45.979
"Who wants to know?"

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.880
"Our fists want to know, you little bastard!"

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:56.659
The guys took the cash and put together a
pilot for Spike TV in 2008 called Mission:

00:01:56.659 --> 00:02:00.569
Uncomfortable that would ultimately become
Impractical Jokers.

00:02:00.569 --> 00:02:06.159
The network ultimately decided not to pick
the show up, but the guys often show snippets

00:02:06.159 --> 00:02:08.020
from it at their live shows.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.729
Joke's on them

00:02:09.729 --> 00:02:14.170
The idea for the pilot came one night when
the guys were discussing prank shows and were

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:19.550
inspired to invert the formula and make an
"upside-down hidden camera show" where they're

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:22.120
the butt of their own jokes.

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:24.400
Or one of their butts is the joke ...

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:30.150
"You have a finger in your a------ on television!"

00:02:30.150 --> 00:02:35.650
The Tenderloins say they don't consider Impractical
Jokers a reality show or even necessarily

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:39.920
a prank show, but instead just a platform
for their comedy.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:45.570
Four dudes wreaking havoc in public sure sounds
like Jackass, but the guys are quick to point

00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:51.400
out that the joke is always on them instead
of the unsuspecting public, or as Joe calls

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.070
them, merely the "instrument of embarrassment."

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:55.780
"Oh my God."

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.200
"That's what happens when you lose!"

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:00.810
Behind the schemes

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:05.900
When Jokers started out, it was literally
just the four guys in control of every aspect

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:12.280
of the show — they even shot their "pitch
episode" for TruTV in 2010 with their iPhones.

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:17.770
After the success of the first two seasons,
they finally were making enough money to hire

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:22.860
some writers to work with them in creating
the jokes and brainstorming ideas for challenges.

00:03:23.980 --> 00:03:26.140
"Low Risk, High Yield Investments.

00:03:26.140 --> 00:03:27.150
Little risk, lotta yield.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.810
You don't put a lot of risk out there, and
you bring a lot of yield in.

00:03:30.810 --> 00:03:32.530
It's all about the yield."

00:03:32.530 --> 00:03:38.720
But that doesn't mean the foursome are hands-off
after six seasons and counting.

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:43.890
Murr describes the show as "an improv show
in disguise," with about 60 to 70 percent

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:45.630
of it made up on the fly.

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:52.170
Joe still oversees all the editing, too, ensuring
The Tenderloins' stamp remains firmly on the

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:54.920
Jokers product no matter how big they get.

00:03:54.920 --> 00:03:56.210
Off-screen antics

00:03:56.210 --> 00:04:01.080
The guys may be getting paid to force each
other into the most excruciating situations

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:05.080
imaginable on TV, but that doesn't mean they
lay off in private.

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:09.790
They play tricks on each other constantly
off-camera, and, much like on the show, Murr

00:04:09.790 --> 00:04:11.640
is typically the target.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:16.200
One of their favorite pranks involves increasing
the tip Murr leaves at restaurants by adding

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:19.450
a digit somewhere on the receipt while he's
distracted.

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:22.820
"Murr's cruise credit card.

00:04:22.820 --> 00:04:26.140
There's money on the boat, you use this to
buy everything, you guys want a drink?"

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:27.980
"Keep them coming!"

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:33.431
The guys rarely dare Joe to do anything in
public anymore, so convinced are they that

00:04:33.431 --> 00:04:37.150
he'll do pretty much anything proposed to
him, just for laughs.

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:41.930
After all, this is the guy who walked down
Wall Street in a wedding dress with his dog

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.740
gussied up as a groom beside him ...

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.660
Jokers in the wild

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:55.690
Far from one-prank ponies, the guys have shared
their comedic talents elsewhere, with Sal

00:04:55.690 --> 00:05:01.690
and Murr doing an episode of Bones in 2015,
and all four scheduled to make appearances

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:04.320
in the Syfy series 12 Monkeys.

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:09.820
Q is set to star in a new movie directed by
indie horror maestro Adam Green, which means

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:13.640
he's likely get his head blown off in some
wonderfully gruesome way.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:19.070
But Murr says their solo interests won't keep
them from their Jokers duties, and the guys

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:22.600
will "keep working together for a long time
to come."

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:23.910
"You guys just f---ed up."

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:26.590
"Hey watch your language buddy, it's a family
friendly show."

00:05:26.590 --> 00:05:30.210
"Season six is coming, and I'm going to destroy
the three of you."

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:31.580
Thanks for watching!

00:05:31.580 --> 00:05:34.870
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:05:34.870 --> 00:05:37.710
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

